      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1786212137725,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "7. {\n    \"fileId\":\"F123\",\n    \"chunkSize\":8388608,\n    \"parts\": [\n    {\n      \"partNumber\": 1,\n      \"url\": \"https://s3....\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"partNumber\": 2,\n      \"url\": \"https://s3....\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"partNumber\": 3,\n      \"url\": \"https://s3....\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "7. {\n    \"fileId\":\"F123\",\n    \"chunkSize\":8388608,\n    \"parts\": [\n    {\n      \"partNumber\": 1,\n      \"url\": \"https://s3....\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"partNumber\": 2,\n      \"url\": \"https://s3....\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"partNumber\": 3,\n      \"url\": \"https://s3....\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "dIa-GzOVdMJafwriqlSEb",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1083.8185810031227,
      "y": 1089.642883634951,
      "width": 1141.280858540064,
      "height": 475,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#e03131",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bDC",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1039004368,
      "version": 1379,
      "versionNonce": 843914960,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1786211423747,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "the s3 multipart url looks like\nhttps://drive-prod.s3.amazonaws.com/objects/93/af/91f823.pdf\n    ?partNumber=7\n    &uploadId=XYZ987\ndrive-prod is bucket can be after / as well \nobjectKey - objects/93/af/91f823.pdf\n\nWhat if someone mofifies the fileName in the url to some malicious thingy, because all validation is done, the x-amz-\nsignature is changed in this case for the url . if url is the same then we need malicious file validation pipeline in some\nsvc which validate and then only mark status of file as READY in file metadata\n\n\nX-AMZ-* is used for request auth/auth\nX-Amz-Algorithm - The alog used to sign the url e,,g AWS4-HMAC-SHA256\nX-Amz-Credential - the identity that signed the url (prod creds for aws s3)\nX-Amz-Date - when record was signed\nX-Amz-Expires=3600\nX-Amz-SignedHeaders Which HTTP headers are included in the cryptographic signature?\nX-Amz-Signature - actual signature",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "the s3 multipart url looks like\nhttps://drive-prod.s3.amazonaws.com/objects/93/af/91f823.pdf\n    ?partNumber=7\n    &uploadId=XYZ987\ndrive-prod is bucket can be after / as well \nobjectKey - objects/93/af/91f823.pdf\n\nWhat if someone mofifies the fileName in the url to some malicious thingy, because all validation is done, the x-amz-signature is changed in this case for the url . if url is the same then we need malicious file validation pipeline in some svc which validate and then only mark status of file as READY in file metadata\n\n\nX-AMZ-* is used for request auth/auth\nX-Amz-Algorithm - The alog used to sign the url e,,g AWS4-HMAC-SHA256\nX-Amz-Credential - the identity that signed the url (prod creds for aws s3)\nX-Amz-Date - when record was signed\nX-Amz-Expires=3600\nX-Amz-SignedHeaders Which HTTP headers are included in the cryptographic signature?\nX-Amz-Signature - actual signature",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
